herolte: scenario bench v3 (0471): app launches and boot-line parameters

min104 (E-0325): the scripted idle costs the same with any cap (the big cores are parked), and what the Director felt as slowness at 1664 MHz was opening apps, which the bench did not play. v3 drops idle from the default set, adds a launch scenario that taps the four dock slots and measures how long iOS keeps drawing after each tap (settle_ms), shortens paging to ten swipes, and reads its configuration from the boot line with defaults that need no argument: heroscenr (rounds, 3), heroscens (scenario mask, 11 = paging, spotlight, launch), heroscenc (cap digits, 432). heroscen only.

--- a/bsd/dev/herolte_gpu.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/herolte_gpu.c
@@ -3049,18 +3049,58 @@
 	return waited;
 }
 
+/* 0471: "launch" scenario and the time an app takes to settle. Taps the four dock slots in turn (Phone, Safari, Messages and the fourth
+ * of the stock layout; an empty slot opens nothing and is reported as -1) and, after each tap, measures how long iOS keeps drawing:
+ * first it waits up to 1.5 s for frames to start, then until fewer than 2 frames per 300 ms (at most 6 s). That time is the closest
+ * thing to "how long the app took to open" that the kernel can see, and it is what the Director felt as slowness at 1664 MHz. */
+static void
+hs_tap(int x, int y)
+{
+	hs_touch(1, x, y);
+	hg_msleep(60);
+	hs_touch(0, x, y);
+}
+
+static int
+hs_settle(unsigned *frames_out)
+{
+	uint64_t f0 = herolte_fb_presented, t0 = mach_absolute_time(), ns = 0;
+	unsigned waited = 0;
+	bool started = false;
+	while (waited < 1500 && !started) {
+		hg_msleep(100);
+		waited += 100;
+		started = (herolte_fb_presented - f0) >= 2;
+	}
+	if (!started) {
+		*frames_out = 0;
+		return -1;
+	}
+	while (waited < 6000) {
+		uint64_t f1 = herolte_fb_presented;
+		hg_msleep(300);
+		waited += 300;
+		if (herolte_fb_presented - f1 < 2) {
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	*frames_out = (unsigned)(herolte_fb_presented - f0);
+	absolutetime_to_nanoseconds(mach_absolute_time() - t0, &ns);
+	return (int)(ns / 1000000);
+}
